# ICU Patient Flow

ICU is a critical-care overlay on the IPD admission, not a separate admission system; keep ICU work focused on the stay, alerts, observations, rounds, and step-down decisions.

## Stage Sequence

- Eligible patient identified from ward, emergency, OPD, or theater.
- ICU bed assigned or reserved and the stay opened on the IPD admission.
- Nursing receive, baseline assessment, and intensive monitoring.
- Critical alerts raised, acknowledged, and resolved.
- Intensivist rounds and inpatient orders continue.
- Step-down or transfer completed; stay ends while the IPD admission stays open.

## Handoffs

- Bed manager and ICU charge nurse own ICU bed placement.
- ICU nurse owns observations, vitals, alerts, and handover support.
- Intensivist owns round notes, treatment changes, and step-down readiness.
- IPD and Discharge own the broader admission and final closure.

## Hard Invariants

- Must attach every ICU record to the same IPD admission.
- Must not duplicate the admission lifecycle in ICU.
- Must not show "patient discharged" until IPD discharge is complete.
- Must not let billing or cashier workflows drive ICU clinical actions.

## Allowed Critical Actions

- Start ICU stay only for eligible admissions with an ICU bed or completed escalation.
- Resolve critical alerts before lower-priority work.
- Use the IPD encounter for all orders and timeline updates.
- End ICU stay after step-down, not before.

